As a type of coordination polymer, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted extensive attention due to their large specific surface area, adjustable pore size and high porosity. Electro-Fenton (EF) technology is an environmentally-friendly and efficient advanced oxidation process (AOPs) degradation technology, which has also received extensive attention. Therefore, the paper systematically reviews the applications of MOFs in EF systems. Firstly, the advantages and disadvantages of homogeneous and heterogeneous EF were generalized, and the influencing factors of EF system (i.e., electrode, pH, current density, conductivity) were summarized. Sec­ ondly, different series of MOFs (i.e., zeolite imidazole framework (ZIF), materials of institute lavoisier frame­ works (MIL) and universitetet i oslo (UIO)) were introduced. Then, the methods to improve the catalytic activity and water stability of MOFs were also described. Finally, the future application of MOFs in EF systems is pro­ spected, which provides a detailed summary and foreshadowing for future research in this direction.
## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of industry and also outbreak of new diseases (such as Covid-19 pandemic), the industrial organic matter contents [1] and pharmaceutical wastes [2] in the water environment increases sharply, thus causing serious pollution of water resources. Traditional treatment techniques, such as microbial degradation [3], have been unable to meet the requirements for the treatment of waste­ water containing highly toxic and refractory organic matter [4]. Therefore, it is necessary to develop a series of efficient water treatment technologies. Advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) can produce hy­ droxyl radicals ( OH), superoxide radicals, singlet oxygen and other highly reactive oxygen species (ROS) [5], which can directly mineralize or decompose most organic matter [6].
According to different reaction conditions, AOPs can be divided into several categories, such as sonochemical oxidation, ozone oxidation, photochemical oxidation, electrochemical oxidation, and Fenton oxidation [7–9]. Among them, the traditional Fenton technology uses $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ to react with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce •OH [10–12], thereby degrading many refractory pollutants [13], bacteria [14] and viruses [15]. How­ ever, due to the large number of reagents needed in its processing pro­ cess and the high cost of preparing and transporting $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , electroFenton (EF) was developed.
Traditional EF also produces OH through $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , but the difference is that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is produced by a cathodic reduction reaction under oxygenation or aeration [16,17]. This technique saves the cost of reagents and allows continuous production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ regener­ ation to ensure high efficiency. It also has the advantages of small footprint, high efficiency and easy automatic control. However, it also has disadvantages, such as the requirement of the EF system to operate under acidic conditions, which limits its application [18].
In order to achieve more efficient degradation and expand pH range, researchers began to include catalysts and modifications to the anodes and cathodes in EF [19]. Many catalysts, such as zero-valent iron (ZVI) [20], carbon nanotubes [21], biochar [22], and zeolite [23], have been applied to catalyze the EF system. However, these catalysts will lose some active sites during the modification processes, and excess iron will precipitate. These processes are not conducive to the degradation of pollutants. So, it is important to develop a series of new catalysts.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of coordination poly­ mers that have developed rapidly in recent decades. They generally use metal ions as the connection points, organic ligands as the supporter to form the space. As a new type of porous material, MOFs have the ad­ vantages of high porosity, low density, large specific surface area, adjustable pore size, and structural diversity [24,25]. Thus, MOFs and its derivatives have been applied to adsorption and catalytic reactions. For example, Zr-MOF was used to adsorb heavy metals [26], NBCO-MOF was used as an efficient catalyst in persulfate degradation of pollutants [27], and there are some kinds of MOFs were used for photocatalytic antibacterial [28,29]. Also, MOFs are commonly used to treat pharma­ ceuticals in wastewater [30]. However, traditional MOFs also have disadvantages, such as poor water stability. Therefore, improving the water stability of MOFs is of great significance for practical applications.
Since MOFs have been found to have good catalytic activity, EF system may need to develop new catalysts, many studies have applied MOFs as an efficient catalyst in EF systems. To the best of our knowl­ edge, few studies have systematically summarized and prospected MOFs as efficient catalysts for EF [31]. However, there is no comprehensive review on improving the catalytic activity and water stability of MOFs in EF. So, in this work, the following aspects are introduced: (1) Homo­ geneous and heterogeneous EF will be presented and their optimal conditions will be listed. (2)  MOFs with different structural classifica­ tions are introduced in detail. (3) Emphasis will be placed on the current research on MOFs as catalysts for EF systems. Methods to improve the stability and catalytic activity of MOFs are summarized in detail, and two ways that currently exist for MOF to catalyze EF systems were listed. (4) Finally, future issues such as improving the catalytic performances of MOFs are prospected, and the future applications of MOFs in EF system are discussed.
## 2. Electro-Fenton
## 2.1. Homogeneous and heterogeneous electro-Fenton
EF systems can be divided into two major categories: homogeneous EF and heterogeneous EF. The traditional catalytic oxidation mechanism of homogeneous EF is as follows [32,33]:
Cathode:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{{\cal O}_{2}+2{\cal H}^{+}+2e^{-}\rightarrow{\cal H}_{2}{\cal O}_{2}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{{\cal H}_{2}{\cal O}_{2}+2{\cal H}^{+}+2e^{-}\rightarrow2{\cal H}_{2}{\cal O}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{{\cal O}_{2}+4{\cal H}^{+}+4e^{-}\rightarrow2{\cal H}_{2}{\cal O}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{{\cal F}e^{3_{+}}+e^{-}\rightarrow{\cal F}e^{2{\bf+\ \ }}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{2{\cal H}^{+}+2e^{-}\rightarrow{\cal H}_{2}}}\end{array}
$$
Anode:
$$
2H_{2}O\rightarrow O_{2}+4H^{+}+4e^{-}
$$
The traditional mechanism of heterogeneous EF catalytic oxidation is as follows: Fe was fixed in the catalyst to replace $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ in the heteroge­ neous Fenton reaction with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , thus degrading the pollutant. There are two types of heterogeneous EF systems: one is that the cathode first produces $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and then the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reacts with the dispersive catalyst in the solution, in which the heterogeneous Fenton catalyst acts as the third electrode. The other is directly supported catalyst on the cathode, which acts as a functional electrode and directly decomposes $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ after generating it.
Compared with the homogeneous EF, the heterogeneous EF usually achieves a higher pollutant degradation rate. This is because in homo­ geneous EF, $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ may bind to intermediates and lead to catalyst inac­ tivation [34]. It also has disadvantages such as a narrow pH range [35]. However, the catalyst with heterogeneous EF can immobilize ions on the matrix [36], so that it can fully exert its catalytic activity. A MIL-53(Fe) modified by reduced sulfur, which can efficiently degrade sulfadi­ methamine (SMT) over a wide pH range (3–9) when the MIL-53 (Fe)/S ratio is 1:2. Moreover, the rate constant of heterogeneous EF is 16 times higher than homogeneous EF at $\mathtt{p H}=7$ [37].
(Mechanistic diagram of traditional EF (with catalyst) is detailed in Fig. 1).
## 2.2. Impact parameters
## 2.2.1. Electrode
The electrode selection is usually very important in EF systems. For the anode, boron-doped diamond (BDD), Pt or Ti electrodes are typically used [38]. Although the performance and efficiency of BDD and Pt electrodes are high, their price is relatively expensive [39]. They are not suitable for large-scale use at present. So, the relatively inexpensive Ti/ $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}–\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ anode has been normally used [40]. For the cathode, the production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the cathodic reduction reaction of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ is very important [41]. At present, the preparation trend of new cathode is: (1) to increase the mass transfer of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to the electrode surface, (2) and to promote two-electron reduction to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . (3) Catalyze the oxi­ dation–reduction reaction of the system with high efficiency. At present, carbon-based materials have become the cathode of choice for EF sys­ tems because of its high porosity structure, large SSA and relatively low cost [42–44].
Modification of anode and cathode is effective to improve the cata­ lytic efficiency of EF. At present, the research on MOF-modified elec­ trodes is mainly focused on MOF-modified cathodes. Researchers usually adopt the coating of MOF and its derivative materials on graphite or carbon felt for modification. For example, a study used graphite felt as a cathode coated with $\mathbf{Mn}/{\mathbf{Co}}$ MOF-74 to degrade cip­ rofloxacin (CIP). The degradation rate of CIP reached $99.8\%$ within 60 min [45]. Another study coated carbon materials with $\mathrm{Ce-MOFs/Fe_{3}O_{4}}$ to degrade sulfamethazine (SMT). The catalyst can provide the EF sys­ tem with an extremely high $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation rate with very low energy consumption [46].
## 2.2.2. pH
The $\mathtt{p H}$ value of the solution has a great influence on the cathodic reduction reaction of oxygen. Typically, a four-electron reduction re­ action of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ occurs in alkaline solutions (equation $(7)$ , whereas a twoelectron reduction reaction to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ occurs only in acidic solutions (equation (8). Therefore, the $\mathfrak{p H}$ of the solution determines the effi­ ciency of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation, affecting the degradation of organic pollutants.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{{\cal O}_{2}+2H_{2}{\cal O}+4e^{-}{\rightarrow}4{\cal O}H^{-}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{{\cal O}_{2}+2H^{+}+2e^{-}{\rightarrow}2H_{2}{\cal O}_{2}}}\end{array}
$$
In the conventional case, the initial $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{pH}}$ of the EF reaction should be less than 3.5 [47]. So, now researchers have been looking for ways to expand the $\mathsf{p H}$ range in EF system. At present, there are studies to expand the $\mathsf{p H}$ range of the reaction by modifying the electrode [48], adding catalysts and other approaches [33]. A MOF-derived hollow sea urchin-shaped carbon anchor mono-atomic iron as a bifunctional EF catalyst, is used to reduce $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and to activate in situ to OH. The catalyst enables the EF system to exhibit fast reaction kinetics at pH 3–11 [49].
## 2.2.3. Current density
The current also has an effect on the reaction rate of EF system [50,51]. However, the amount of current passing per unit area is limited. When the current per unit area increases, the polarization of the elec­ trode increases, and the side reaction of cathode and anode will occur [52]. The current density of the anode increases, $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ will be precipitation (equation (9). The current density of the cathode increases, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ will be precipitated (equation (10). Moreover, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ precipitation will lead to an increase in the pH value of the solution, which is not conducive to free radical reaction [53]. For example, when $\mathtt{p H}$ is 3, carbon felt is used as the cathode, BDD is used as the anode, and the current density is $60\mathrm{mA}/\$ $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , chloroquine can be completely removed [54]. When CMIL-88(Fe)– $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was used as the catalyst for electro-Fenton degradation of herbi­ cides, the current density measured at the ORR peak was $2.3~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ [55].
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{H_{2}O+4e^{-}\rightarrow O_{2}+4H^{+}}}\\ {{{}}}\\ {{2H_{2}O+2e^{-}\rightarrow H_{2}+2O H^{-}}}\end{array}
$$
## 2.2.4. The conductivity of the solution
Due to the poor electrical conductivity of organic wastewater, an appropriate amount of electrolyte should be added when electro­ chemical reactions occur. In the choice of electrolyte, the inorganic re­ agent should be environmentally friendly, not produce toxic substances, and possess strong conductivity [56]. As the concentration increases, the conductivity of the $\mathrm{Na_{2}s O_{4}}$ solution increases rapidly, and it also does not participate in the reaction. One experiment investigated the effect of three electrolytes (0.05 M $\mathrm{Na_{2}s O_{4}}$ , 0.05 M NaCl, 0.05 M ${\bf N a N O}_{3})$ on electro-Fenton degradation and found that the highest removal rate was achieved using 0.05 M $\mathrm{Na_{2}s O_{4}}$ [57]. Moreover, some studies have found that Cl- is a hydroxyl radical scavenger, which can significantly delay the oxidation reaction [58]. Therefore, 0.05 M $\mathrm{Na_{2}S O_{4}}$ was generally selected as the electrolyte [59].
Although the current density and electrolyte solution of EF system are not directly related to the catalysis of MOFs in EF, they still affect the final degradation efficiency of EF system, so it is generally described here.
## 3. Classification of MOFs
(Only the MOF associated with EF was involved).
## 3.1. ZIF series
Zeolite imidazole framework (ZIF) is a zeolite-like MOF material synthesized by the reaction of Zn or Co with imidazole [60]. It is attached to the transition metal by organic imidazole cross-linking, thus forming a polyhedral framework [61]. Also, it has a rich pore cage structure [62]. Its unsaturated coordination metal nodes, imidazole li­ gands and guest molecules in the pore can act as the catalytic active center [63]. The M IM M bond Angle of ZIFs is $145^{\circ}$ , which is equal to the Si-O-Si bond Angle in the molecular sieve [64]. Thus, ZIFs exhibit similar porous structure to zeolites, while exhibiting better chemical stability (compared to other MOFs) and thermal stability in organic solution. By changing the synthesis process and conditions, ZIFs with the same composition but different topological structures can be obtained [65]. They have attracted attention due to characteristics such as structural flexibility, adjustable frame and aperture [66].
ZIF-8 is the most common ZIF, widely used as an adsorbent and catalyst. Its skeleton is composed of 2-methylimidazole and $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms. During the preparation of ZIF-8, metal ions will coordinate with organic solvents such as methanol or DMF, resulting in the formation of unsat­ urated metal coordination sites [67]. These unsaturated metal coordi­ nation sites can promote the catalytic reaction. Its preparation methods mainly include solvothermal synthesis [68], microwave-assisted [69] and microfluidic methods [70]. Among them, solvothermal synthesis is the most widely used [68]. It dissolves ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and 2-methylimidazole in water or organic solvents, and directly synthesizes ZIF-8 by heating the solution. First, 2-methylimidazole is deprotonated to form a crystal seed with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ . Then, the excess 2-methylimidazole adsorbed on the posi­ tively charged ZIF-8 surface and terminated its growth. Although this method is convenient, it takes a long time to react, resulting in a waste of reagents and high energy consumption. Microwave-assisted synthesis has the advantage of easy control of heat and mass transfer. To overcome the shortcomings, microwave-assisted solvothermal synthesis was found [71]. ZIF-8, which was synthesized by a simple and efficient method, has a similar topological structure of sodium silicate, thermal stability and hydrophobic micropores [72]. In one study, ZIF-8 was carbonized to form carbon nanotubes, which formed three-dimensional porous elec­ trodes in an EF system, and was found to be effective in removing a variety of organic pollutants [73]. In addition, Fe-ZIF-8 in the presence of HEF system $(\mathrm{pH}=7$ , catalyst concentration of $0.2\ \mathrm{g\L^{-1}}$ , current of $100\mathrm{\mA},$ achieved $99\%$ removal of Phenazopyridine Hydrochloride [74]. The flower-like nanostructure of $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}@\mathrm{ZNC}$ has good redox ca­ pacity due to its ability to expose multi-edge active sites. It degraded phenol with $98.8\%$ efficiency within $120\mathrm{min}$ [75].
ZIF-67 is a cobalt-based zeolite imidazole ester skeleton structure material. It is formed by the self-assembly of divalent cobalt ions in combination with 2-methylimidazole. It also has the characteristics of porous structure, large specific surface area (SSA), which can be applied to catalysis and adsorption [76]. Its most beneficial advantage for cat­ alysing is that it can obtain magnetic metal Co after high temperature treatment, and can form a porous carbon skeleton structure [77]. Based on the excellent structure of ZIF-67, ZIF-67/Ni-Co layered double hy­ droxide precursor was thermolysis, and prepared a novel double-shell hollow structure catalyst. Due to its complex hollow structure, it has good electrochemical properties. Also, the lone electron pairs of pyri­ dine N and graphite N in the catalyst can form a delocalized conjugate system with the $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ hybrid carbon skeleton to improve the electro­ catalytic performance of the cathode. Moreover, the presence of $\mathsf{C o-N_{X}}$ reduces the aggregation of Co particles in the reaction. The degradation rate of levodopa reached $99.07\%$ when the catalyst was applied to the EF systems [78].
## 3.2. MIL series
Materials of Institute Lavoisier frameworks (MIL) can be divided into two categories: (1) MOFs of lanthanide and transition metal elements synthesized with binary carboxylic acids. (2) MOFs of Trivalent chro­ mium, iron, aluminum and other metals synthesized with carboxylic acids. Its common synthesis methods are solvothermal synthesis [79], liquid diffusion method [80] and microwave radiation synthesis method [81]. Compared with traditional graphite materials, MIL series materials also have the advantages of porous, large SSA and unsaturated metal coordination sites [82]. The Langmuir specific surface areas of MIL-100 and MIL-101 were up to $3100~\mathrm{{m}}^{2}/\mathrm{{g}}$ and $5900~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively [83].
Due to their large SSA and a high density of transition metal sites, MIL materials are often applied in catalytic and adsorption systems. MIL100, one of the most common materials in the MIL series, was prepared as a new type of catalyst and adsorbent. MIL-100 (Fe) has two meso­ porous cage-cavity structures of different sizes, and the windows are all micropores. Currently, it has been reported that after MIL-100 (Fe) activation, the water molecule at the end of the ferric trimer in the crystal structure will leave, resulting in the formation of ferric Lewis acid sites [84], thus enhanced catalytic activity and expanded catalytic range. So, it is often used in the adsorption of gases and heavy metals. In addition, it has also been applied to catalytic degradation systems. In one study, pyridine N and pyrrole N groups were introduced into a new catalyst prepared by MIL-100 (Fe), and good catalytic performance was achieved in EF systems [85].
## 3.3. UIO series
Universitetet i Oslo (UIO) -MOFs are 3D porous materials con­ structed by $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ and dicarboxylic acid ligands. It has many active binding sites in organic ligands. Due to the strong binding ability of Zr-O bond, the synthesis of UIO-MOFs gradually focuses on functionalization or changing the ligand length, thereby regulating its physicochemical properties and pore size [86].
UIO-66 is composed of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ and has six $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ ions in the octahedral geometry, and it has four oxygen atoms or hydroxy groups in the center of each face. These nodes coordinate with 12 terephthalic acid ligands. Each Zr atom is coordinated with eight oxygen atoms in a square antiprism geometry. The surface area of UIO-66 varies depending on the preparation method, and can reach approximately $1200~\mathrm{{m}}^{2}/\mathrm{{g}}$ [87]. When UIO-66 is heated at high temperatures, even though dehydration occurs around $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ , the structure does not collapse [88]. UIO-66 is stable over a wide range of pH [89], and it is also stable under corrosive reagents such as HCl [90]. In addition, when UIO-66 was modified with the active functional group, it still retained high activity [91]. Due to the high valence state $z\mathrm{r}$ of zirconium metal, it helps to generate strong forces at the metal center and the ligand bond, which stabilizes the material [63]. Due to its significant thermal and water stability, it is often used in catalysis under various conditions. For example, when $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and Pd/UIO-66 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ are added to the Fenton system, it is found that sulfadiazine degradation efficiency can reach more than $98\%$ after 180 min, which is significantly better than the efficiency of the traditional Fenton systems [92]. Because UIO-66 cannot directly catalyze the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ the establishment of dual active centers is an effective approach. It is also a good loading platform for transition metals. For example, in one study, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was loaded on UIO-66, so that $z\mathrm{r}$ interacted with Fe to form a double active center, which was conducive to the reduction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [93]. Due to the UIO- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot66^{\prime}s$ unique construction, it can withstand a large number of defects without struc­ tural collapse. Therefore, future studies can focus on the combination of UIO-66 with defect engineering to improve its catalytic activity.
