## 4. Catalyst
Methods to improve the catalytic activity and water stability of MOFs in EF system are detailed in Fig. 3.
## 4.1. Improve the stability of MOFs in EF
In EF catalysis, MOFs usually need to be in an aqueous environment. However, in the aqueous environment, the organic ligands of MOFs are easy to be gradually replaced by water molecules, which eventually leads to the collapse of the stock price. Therefore, how to improve water stability is a challenge for MOFs to enter practical applications.
(1) Formation of hydrophobic ligands helps to improve the water stability of MOFs. Binding hydrophobic groups with linkers or establishing hydrophobic groups around metal cluster junctions are both ways to reduce the affinity of MOFs toward water, thereby enhancing the hydrophobicity of MOFs. Because the hydrophobic groups around the metal clusters are able to protect weak coordination bonds and thus reduce the attack of water molecules. Methyl groups [94], fluoroalkyl groups [95], etc., can modify MOF and form good water stability. A series of poly MOFs have also been developed, and some poly MOFs also have good hydrophobicity [96].   
(2) Low-valent metals can improve the stability of MOFs, such as $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ [97–99]. Low-valent metal ions have high alkalinity and thus a water-stable backbone of the ni­ trogen donor linker. For example, the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions of ZIF-8 are composed by imidazole salt linkage and are very stable in the aqueous environment [100]. Low-valent metal ions and triazolyl linker assembly can also improve the water stability of MOFs [101]. However, such MOFs are less resistant to acidic solutions because of the strong affinity between protons and azole salt groups.
(3) Bimetallic MOFs improve not only catalytic activity, but also water stability. Because it can form stronger coordination bonds and enhance the metal cluster inertia [102]. It has been experimentally found that partially substituted Fe-HKUST-1 MOFs have greater surface area retention and little crystallinity loss compared to the original HKUST-1 MOF. Moreover, Fe sub­ stitution at $5\mathrm{mol}\%$ significantly improved the water stability of HKUST-1 MOFs [103].
(4) Surface hydrophobic coating of synthesized MOFs is also a method to improve the water stability of MOFs. As a common hydrophobic coating material, organosilicon is widely used in improving the water stability of MOF. In one study, MOF powder was dissolved in an organosilicon solution to form a surface hy­ drophobic coating at room temperature [104]. Coating the sur­ face of MOFs with polymer layers has also been widely used. For example, it has been studied to form a hydrophobic polymer layer on the surface of HKUST-1 by 2, 2, 2-trifluoroethyl methacrylate and 3-methylacryloyloxypropyl trimethoxylsilane. The hydro­ phobic polymer is able to protect the weak metal ligand bonds from the attack of water molecules, and the MOF shows a water contact angle of $\sim135^{\circ}$ after coating with this coating, so it has high water stability [105]. The original DUT-4 had extremely poor water stability and almost lost its porosity after 6 d of exposure. However, after creating a hydrophobic layer on the DUT-4 surface, the DUT-4 basically maintained its structure and morphology under the same exposure [106]. Thus, hydrophobic treatment broadens the application range of MOF in aqueous environments.
(5) MOFs intersect other materials and may enhance the water sta­ bility of MOFs. Because MOFs grow on the surface or inside the pores of other materials, or other materials are added to the pores of the materials, the synthesized composite can make the com­ posite have the combined characteristics of the two components. For example, adding carbon nanotubes (CNTs) [107] and poly­ mers [108] can enhance the stability of MOFs. In a previous study, composite materials were formed by in situ polymerization and encapsulation of ionic liquids (lLs) in the pores of MOFs [109]. The polymerization of lLs improved active sites via the formation of internal ionic fillers, leading to good stability both for catalysis and adsorption.
## 4.2. Improve the catalytic activity of MOFs in EF
Due to the advantages of MOFs, more and more studies have applied MOFs to EF systems. In order to achieve better catalytic effect, the cat­ alytic activity of MOFs in EF must be improved. Herein, methods to improve the catalytic activity of MOFs in EF are summarized.
(3) Composite-W-based particles combined with graphene or carbon nanotubes can also play a facilitating role in EF catalysis. This method allows the fine-tuning of electrical conductivity. Gra­ phene, as a single layer of graphite sheet composed of carbon atoms through $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ hybridization, is a two-dimensional carbon material [121]. CNTs are thin sheets of graphene coiled into barrels [122]. The carbon atoms in graphene form six-membered rings, which are tightly packed in a honeycomb shape, making it the thinnest and stiffest two-dimensional material found so far. It has good conductive properties [123]. It also has the advantages of large SSA, high mechanical strength and chemical stability. Therefore, it has been widely used as a support for heterogeneous catalysts and immobilized metal complex catalysts [124]. For example, an MOF nanosheet based on highly porous conductive graphene (PCG) was found to be an efficient, economical and durable electrocatalyst for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) [125]. But literature on applying graphene-based MOF materials in EF catalysis is still limited which requires future studies.
(4) Ligand-defect engineering is a way to improve the catalytic ac­ tivity of MOFs. It has several advantages for enhancing the cat­ alytic activity of MOFs: (1) the surface pore size and properties can be tuned. (2) Increase the number of Lewis acid centers of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ to improve the catalytic activity. (3) The introduction of ligand vacancies in MOFs can enhance the ability of electron-hole separation [126]. A defective engineered zirconium tricarbox­ ylate MOF-808 compound (DE-MOF-808) was prepared by mix­ ing tricarboxylate ligands with dicarboxylate ligands, and the defective MOF had good catalytic performance in the reduction of carbonyl compounds [127]. Studies have found that within a certain range, the larger the pore size, the higher the catalytic activity [128]. In one study, a defect engineering approach was used to adjust the aperture of $\mathrm{MOF}{-}808{-}S0_{4}$ . Among them, the aperture of MO $\mathrm{F}{-}808{-}5\%{-}S0_{4}$ is $1.46~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , M $)\mathrm{F}{-}808{-}10\%{-}S($ O4 is $1.53~\mathrm{nm}$ , MOF-808–15%-SO4 is $1.99\ \mathrm{nm}$ , MO $\mathrm{F}–808–20\%.8$ O4 is $2.25~\mathrm{nm}$ . Moreover, it was found that when the pore size was $1.4\mathrm{-}2.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , the pore size was linearly related to the catalytic activity, that is, the concentration of defective ligand was linearly related to the catalytic activity. M $\mathsf{I O F-808{-}15^{\circ}/\mathrm{{0-}}}\mathsf{S O}$ 4 yielded the highest yield of the target product [129]. Therefore, it is very important to find the appropriate concentration of defective li­ gands for the catalytic activity of MOF. Due to the high sensitivity of spectral characterization methods to the chemical and elec­ tronic structures of materials, So, defect concentration can be analyzed through spectral characterization results (such as Raman shift ID/IG, metal atom coordination number changes in EXAFS data, etc.) [130]. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is also a method to assess the presence of defects and is widely used due to its easy availability. One study used TGA to find about one to three intrinsic deletions of 12 connectors in each cluster of UIO-66 [131]. The TGA binding energy with MS provides infor­ mation about the type and number of guest molecules incorpo­ rated [132]. The application of defective ligand engineering in different MOFs is detailed in Table. 2.
(5) Bimetallic active sites have better catalytic performance than single metal active sites. Doping two different metal atoms into MOFs can both increase the density of active site and different metals can also produce a synergistic effect to enhance the cat­ alytic activity of the catalyst [133]. Also, the abundant electron environment can well promote electron transfer. The application of different bimetallic MOFs in EF is detailed in Table. 3.   
(6) Heat transfer can affect the catalytic activity of MOFs. The heat transfer of MOFs has attracted some attention in recent years [134]. Excessive high temperature may lead to deactivation of MOFs catalyst. Therefore, the preparation of MOFs with high thermal conductivity is also a condition to ensure its catalytic activity. For example, one study synthesized a series of $\mathrm{{siO}_{2}}$ modified $\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}@\mathsf{A l}$ composites with high thermal conductivity. Compared with the catalyst with low thermal conductivity, it was found that the bed temperature of the catalyst was easier to reach the steady state when changing the oven temperature [135].
