The application of MOFs as catalysts for EF systems is generally divided into two categories: (1) modified cathodic catalyst and (2) suspended particle catalysts. However, the current research mainly stays in the modified cathodic catalyst, and the research on suspended particle catalyst is insufficient. Two catalytic methods are summarized in detail in Table. 4. The preparation process of MOFs modified cathode is shown in Fig. 4.
## 5. Conclusion and future perspective
MOFs have the advantages of large specific surface area, high porosity and adjustable pore size, and EF system is an efficient and environmental-friendly advanced oxidation technology. Nowadays, MOFs are widely used in EF degradation. The advantages and disad­ vantages of homogeneous and heterogeneous EF systems are analyzed and compared in detail. From the comparison, it can be seen that het­ erogeneous EF is more widely used at present. The influencing factors of EF system were summarized, such as electrode material selection, pH, current density, and electrolyte selection. MOFs were classified into ZIF, IRMOF, MIL, UIO, PCN series. The ZIF and MIL series have been used for catalysis in EF systems far more than the other series. Finally, the ap­ plications of different MOFs in EF are reviewed. Methods to improve the water stability of MOFs were summarized. Among them, the formation of hydrophobic groups and hydrophobic coating on the surface of MOFs are the most conventional and effective.  Introducing dopants and defective structures, forming nanostructures, coupling MOFs with car­ bon nanotubes and other materials, and forming bimetallic active sites are all good approaches to improving the catalytic activity of MOFs. In recent years, two types of MOFs catalysis in EF system, modified cathodic catalyst and suspended particle catalyst, have been summa­ rized and listed.
The current application of MOFs in EF system is prospected as follows:
(1) For EF systems, finding suitable electrodes is the top priority. Although BDD is efficient, it is expensive and not suitable for large-scale industrial use. Therefore, it is essential to develop cost-effective and efficient electrodes.   
(2) Selective degradation of pollutants by electro-Fenton is also an important research direction. For example, $^10_{2}$ in water is highly selective and can degrade pollutants such as organic dyes in a directed manner. So, future studies can target the binding of MOFs and EF system to excite specific free radicals, thereby achieving directed degradation.   
(3) At present, the preparation of MOFs is a challenge. Although there are many methods to prepare MOFs, such as solvothermal synthesis, microwave-assisted synthesis, optimal energy con­ sumption, minimal synthesis duration and maximum yield should be explored to reduce chemical/energy input.   
(4) So far, ZIF and MIL have been used in EF systems. But the remaining series of MOFs, such as IRMOF, are usually applied to storage gases. Therefore, a variety of series of MOFs can be tried in EF. In addition, suspended particle catalyst is less studied at present, which can be paid attention to in the future.   
(5) Increasing the catalytic activity of MOF in EF systems remains a major focus. The catalytic activity can be increased by increasing the population of the active site in MOF The catalytic activity of MOF mainly depends on the adsorption and desorption ability of the intermediate at the active site, and the adsorption and desorption ability of the intermediate is related to the geometric structure of the MOF. Also, the regulation of defect engineering is also a concern to increase the catalytic activity of MOF. To better study the catalytic activities of MOF, the intrinsic activity dif­ ferences of different monatomic metal species should be investi­ gated. It is also crucial to regulate the appropriate structure and
morphology of the MOF. Some MOFs and its derivatives with high-density active sites, super-exposed nanostructures and graded porous structures should be developed, thus hus maxi­ mizing the use of the active site. Moreover, the water stability of MOF is poor. Therefore, it is important to improve the water stability of MOF. For example, to develop a more stable and faster surface polymerization strategy, coating hydrophobic polymers on the surface of MOF. In addition, methods such as modification of hydrophobic groups in ligands and the use of inert metal clusters can still be studied in depth.
